# Episode 3 - No, Seriously, We Need To Talk About The Plot
## Writing Exercise: Freewriting
### Alice's Writing:
It turns out that sitting down and trying to write for a set amount of time is really hard. She sat there looking at her keyboard knowing that she needed to write something. She had a deadline coming up and if she didn’t get this done today it was going to be to late. Sighing she turned towards her window and gazed out of it. The sun was bright and the birds were loud as ever.

She would much rather be walking around outside, preferably in under trees so that the dappled light would shine in ways that let her imagination actually flow. She remembered the stories that she would make up with her family when they went on walks. Stories of Faires and trolls that would hide behind trees and in pools of water. She longed to return to those days. When time passing didn’t matter and she could play in piles of leaves without a care for who saw her.

She turned back to her computer and sighed again. The essay she was supposed to write was tedious and boring. Something about some book she was supposed to have read for her English class that she may have decided to ignore in order to go out drinking with her friends. That night was fun, or at least what she could remember was fun, which was only some of it.

She stood, turned and left her room. Nothing was going to get written within the next hour, at least nothing she could actually use. Instead, she grabbed her pencils and paper and wandered out the door to the grass outside. Thinking it would be a better use of time she decided that she would try and draw the place she missed. The tall trees with spring green leaves. The forest floor covered in bluebells, with brilliant purples and blues that were shining in the light.

She set quietly and draw from memory a place that she had not been to in years. There was no point in going back to that forest for all the magic that had been there in her childhood had long since left.  Her parents had moved abroad and barely spoke to her, and she was here. Procrastinating writing and essay that was worth half her grade. Not that she cared.

Her pencil moved across the paper with little thought behind it. Just sketching what her minds eye saw, thought often adding in little fae creatures that she never saw but always wished to as a child. She often drew as an escape from reality. As she could add whatever creature she wanted to.

